question:
Eight units each with a reliability of 0.85 are connected in a parallel configuration. At least six units are required for system success. Using the m-out-of-n system reliability formula

R_system = sum_{i=m}^{n} C(n,i) R^i (1-R)^{n-i}

where C(n,i) = n!/(i!(n-i)!) and R is the reliability of each unit, find the reliability of the system. (Here n = 8, m = 6, R = 0.85, and 0! = 1.)

A Weibull probability plot for a failure-time dataset gives an estimated scale parameter η ≈ 900 hours (η is the time at which reliability R = e^{-1} ≈ 0.368, which corresponds to unreliability 0.632 on the plot). The Weibull reliability function is
R(t) = exp[ - (t/η)^β ].
From the plot, the vertical line at t = 200 hours crosses the best-fit line at an unreliability of about 0.14, so the reliability at 200 hours is about 0.86. Using these graphical estimates (η ≈ 900 and R(200) ≈ 0.86), find:
a) the Weibull reliability function (determine β and write R(t));
b) the reliability at 200 hours (compare with the graphical value);
c) the time at which reliability is 0.95.

(a) If the lower and upper 0.90 confidence limits on the mean time between failures (MTBF) for an item are 2395 hours and 9195 hours, respectively, find the lower and upper 0.90 confidence limits on its reliability for any 100-hour interval. Use the exponential reliability model R(t) = exp(-t / MTBF).

(b) Twenty-one thousand device-hours (total time on test) are accumulated in a time-terminated test with seven failures. Find the lower and upper one-sided limits on MTBF with 0.99 confidence. Use the provided chi-square critical values χ^2_{0.01;16} = 32.000 and χ^2_{0.99;14} = 4.660 and the formulas used below:
MTBF(l) = 2T / χ^2_{0.01;16}
MTBF(u) = 2T / χ^2_{0.99;14}
where T = 21,000 and r = 7.

Sixteen thousand device-hours (total time on test), T = 16000, are accumulated in a failure-terminated (type 2) test with r = 4 failures. For an exponential life distribution, the total time on test V = T has the property that 2V/θ is distributed as χ^2 with ν = 2r degrees of freedom. One-sided 100(1 − α)% confidence limits for the mean life θ are given by

- Lower one-sided 100(1 − α)% confidence limit: θ_L = 2T / χ^2_{1−α, 2r}
- Upper one-sided 100(1 − α)% confidence limit: θ_U = 2T / χ^2_{α, 2r}

Find the upper and lower one-sided 90% confidence limits for θ (i.e., use one-sided confidence level 90%, so α = 0.10). Use the χ^2 critical values for ν = 2r = 8: χ^2_{0.90,8} = 13.362 and χ^2_{0.10,8} = 3.490.

Compute the reliability of an active redundant configuration system in which the system succeeds if at least k of n identical components succeed. For a k-out-of-n active redundancy with identical component reliability R, the system reliability is

R_system = sum_{i=k}^{n} C(n,i) R^{i} (1-R)^{n-i}

Compute the system reliability for the specific case n = 3 and k = 2 (i.e., a 2-out-of-3 system), where all three units have identical reliability R.

Sixteen thousand device-hours (total time on test) are accumulated in a failure-terminated test, with four failures. (a) What are the upper and lower one-sided 90% confidence limits on the mean time between failures (MTBF), θ? (b) What are the one-sided 90% confidence limits on reliability R(t) for a 100-hour period, where for an exponential lifetime distribution R(t) = exp(-t/θ)?

Use the following sampling result for a failure-terminated test with r observed failures and total time on test T: 2T/θ ~ χ^2_{2r}. From this, one-sided 100(1−α)% confidence bounds for θ are
- lower one-sided 100(1−α)% bound: θ ≥ 2T / χ^2_{1−α, 2r}
- upper one-sided 100(1−α)% bound: θ ≤ 2T / χ^2_{α, 2r}

A large program is assumed to include a total of N = 300 errors. After t' = 20 hours of execution time, i = 250 errors have been detected and corrected. Under the Littlewood model, the reliability over a further time t is given by

R(t) = ((β + t') / (β + t' + t))^{(N - i) α}

where α and β are distribution parameters. Given α = 0.005, β = 4, and t = 20 (the further 20 hours), what is the expected reliability R(20) over this further 20 hours?

A type of roller bearing has a time to failure which (for the original lubricant) had been estimated from a sample of n1 = 60 to have mean μ1 = 6000 h and sample SD s1 = 450 h. A new lubricant was tested with a sample of n2 = 9 giving mean μ2 = 6400 h and sample SD s2 = 380 h. Using a two-sample test for the difference of means (assuming the sampling distributions of the means are approximately normal and using the sample SDs to estimate variability), compute:

(a) the z-statistic for the difference in sample means using the standard error of the difference SE = sqrt( s1^2/n1 + s2^2/n2 );

(b) state whether the observed difference is statistically significant (i.e., provides sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis of equal means) at conventional significance levels (use the normal distribution).

A space vehicle requires three out of its four main engines to operate in order to achieve orbit. Each engine operates independently and has a reliability (probability of operating) R = 0.97. Using the binomial model where the probability of exactly x components operating is
P(x) = C(n,x) R^x (1-R)^{n-x}
with C(n,x) = n!/(x!(n-x)!), determine the reliability R_s of achieving orbit (the probability that at least k = 3 engines operate out of n = 4).

Given a Weibull failure distribution with shape parameter β = 1/3 and scale parameter θ = 16000, completely characterize the failure process. Use the Weibull formulas:
- Reliability (survival) function: R(t) = exp(−(t/θ)^β)
- Probability density function: f(t) = (β/θ) (t/θ)^{β−1} exp(−(t/θ)^β), t ≥ 0
- Hazard (failure) rate: λ(t) = (β/θ) (t/θ)^{β−1}
- Mean (MTTF): E[T] = θ Γ(1 + 1/β)
- Variance: Var(T) = θ^2 { Γ(1 + 2/β) − [Γ(1 + 1/β)]^2 }
- Median: t_med = θ (−ln 0.5)^{1/β} = θ (ln 2)^{1/β}
- Mode: t_mode = θ (1 − 1/β)^{1/β} for β > 1; t_mode = 0 for β ≤ 1
Also use Γ(n) = (n − 1)! for positive integer n.

Consider the confidence interval for the reliability R(t0) based on n units with r observed failures by time t0:

Pr{R_L ≤ R(t0) ≤ R_U} = 1 − α

where

R_L = (1 + ((r + 1)/(n − r)) · F_2)^{−1}

R_U = F_1 · (F_1 + (r/(n − r + 1)))^{−1}

F_1 = F_{α/2, 2n − 2r + 2, 2r} and F_2 = F_{α/2, 2r + 2, 2n − 2r}

and F_{α/2, n1, n2} is the value from the F-distribution with n1 and n2 degrees of freedom having upper-tail probability α/2.

Specifications call for an engine to have reliability 0.95 at 1000 operating hours. The oldest n = 50 engines in the fleet have just passed 1000 hr with r = 1 failure observed. Using a 95% confidence lower bound (one-sided), is the specification R(1000) ≥ 0.95 being met?

Seventy compressors are observed at 5-month intervals (interval width = 5 months). The observed number of failures in the successive intervals (0–5, 5–10, 10–15, 15–20, 20–25, 25–30, 30–35) months are: 3, 7, 8, 9, 13, 18, 12. Let n = 70, t0 = 0, and define the interval midpoints r̄_i = (t_i + t_{i+1})/2. Use the following estimators (for interval i from t_i to t_{i+1}):

- Estimated reliability at time t_i: R̂(t_i) = n_i / n where n_i is the number surviving at time t_i (n_0 = n).
- Estimated failure density on interval (t_i, t_{i+1}): f̂(t) = (n_i - n_{i+1}) / ((t_{i+1} - t_i) * n).
- Estimated hazard (failure) rate on interval (t_i, t_{i+1}): λ̂(t) = (n_i - n_{i+1}) / ((t_{i+1} - t_i) * n_i).
- Estimated mean time to failure (MTTF): M̂TTF = Σ r̄_i * ((n_i - n_{i+1})/n).
- Sample variance: s^2 = Σ r̄_i^2 * ((n_i - n_{i+1})/n) - M̂TTF^2.

Compute and report, in this order:
1) R̂(t) at t = 0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 months;
2) f̂(t) for intervals (0–5), (5–10), (10–15), (15–20), (20–25), (25–30), (30–35);
3) λ̂(t) for the same seven intervals;
4) the sample mean time to failure M̂TTF;
5) the sample standard deviation s.

Case 2 (Burn-in testing). A new fuel pump has a 25-hour warranty and high infant mortality. Each warranty failure costs the company $1200. To evaluate a burn-in program, 25 pumps were Type I tested for 500 operating hours; 16 failures occurred at times (hr): 0.005, 0.1, 0.2, 1.0, 1.0, 2.3, 9.3, 10.1, 14.2, 16.4, 29.7, 155.2, 172.6, 393.1, 442.8, 445.0. A Weibull distribution with decreasing failure rate was fitted. Maximum likelihood estimates for Type I data gave beta-hat = 0.31 and theta-hat = 461, so the reliability function is R(t) = exp[-(t/461)^{0.31}]. The company considers a 5-hour burn-in. The cost model for burn-in T (hours) and warranty life (25 hr) is
E(C) = 50 T + 350[1 - exp(-(T/461)^{0.31})] + 1200[exp(-(T/461)^{0.31}) - exp(-((T+25)/461)^{0.31})].
Using this model and direct search at 0.1-hr intervals, the E(C) values near the minimum were found with a minimum at T = 0.5 hr giving E(C) = 330.3. Compute:
a) probability of failure during the 25-hr warranty without burn-in, 1 - R(25);
b) conditional reliability R(25 | 5) when a 5-hr burn-in is applied (R(25 | 5) = R(30)/R(5));
c) probability of failure under warranty after 5-hr burn-in;
d) optimal burn-in time T that minimizes E(C) (as found by the direct search) and the corresponding E(C);
e) probability of failure under warranty when T = 0.5 hr.

Case 3 (Preventive maintenance). A turbine steam generator must have reliability 0.98 over 1000 cycles. The first 40 generators failed at cycles (list): 347, 396, 433, 513, 624, 673, 1008, 1035, 1055, 1066, 1162, 1266, 1298, 1361, 1367, 1549, 1561, 1576, 1708, 1834, 1840, 2497, 2554, 2656, 2666, 2686, 3261, 3278, 3281, 3338, 3421, 4238, 4242, 4481, 4845, 5022, 5744, 6013, 6238, 13446. The Weibull (two-parameter) was rejected by Mann's test, and a lognormal fit was accepted (Lilliefors test) with MLEs t_med-hat = 1939.8 and s-hat = 0.83. The reliability function is R(t) = 1 - Phi[ (ln(t / 1939.8)) / 0.83 ]. Compute:
a) R(1000);
b) show that without preventive maintenance the specification is not met;
c) determine a preventive maintenance interval T (cycles) to meet the 0.98 reliability over 1000 cycles by using the model R_m(nT) = R(T)^n where n = 1000/T (choose integer n so that nT = 1000) and the table values below:
For T=1000 (n=1): R(T)=0.78881, R(T)^n=0.78881
For T=500 (n=2): R(T)=0.9485, R(T)^n=0.8997
For T=250 (n=4): R(T)=0.9932, R(T)^n=0.9730
For T=200 (n=5): R(T)=0.9969, R(T)^n=0.9860
For T=125 (n=8): R(T)=0.9995, R(T)^n=0.9960
State the recommended preventive maintenance interval T and the resulting R_m(1000).

Case 4 (Reliability allocation). A high-frequency radio receiver consists of three serial subsystems: power supply (component 1), tuner (component 2), and receiver (component 3). Accelerated Type I life tests were run (8000 hr test time). Weibull least-squares fits (with F(t_i) = (i-0.3)/(n+0.4)) produced estimated Weibull parameters:
Component 1 (power): beta = 3.98, theta = 10,981;
Component 2 (tuner): beta = 0.986, theta = 22,172;
Component 3 (receiver): beta = 0.928, theta = 3893.5.
Thus R_i(t) = exp[-(t/theta_i)^{beta_i}]. Allowing redundancy, system reliability is
R_s(t) = product_{i=1..3} { 1 - [1 - R_i(t)]^{n_i} }
where n_i is number of parallel units of component i. Unit costs are: power supply $175, tuner $250, receiver $525. Determine the least-cost integer redundancy configuration that achieves R_s(1000) >= 0.95. A search produced the table of candidate configurations and their R_s(1000) and costs; the minimum-cost acceptable configuration found was n1=1, n2=2, n3=3 with R_s(1000)=0.9828358 and total cost $2250. State:
a) the chosen redundancy configuration (n1,n2,n3);
b) the total cost;
c) the achieved R_s(1000).

Case 5 (Reliability growth testing). Prototype road-sensing suspension sensors: 43 sensors tested, six failures at times (hr) 24, 72, 102, 168, 216, 250. Each failure prompted design corrections; surviving units were tested for 300 hr each. The cumulative test time at each failure was computed as:
- at 24 hr failure: 43 * 24 = 1032 hr;
- at 72 hr failure: 1032 + 42*(72-24) = 3048 hr;
- at 102 hr failure: 3048 + 41*(102-72) = 4278 hr;
- at 168 hr failure: 4278 + 40*(168-102) = 6918 hr;
- at 216 hr failure: 6918 + 39*(216-168) = 8790 hr;
- at 250 hr failure: 8790 + 38*(250-216) = 10082 hr.
Remaining 37 units tested to 300 hr gives total cumulative test time 10082 + 37*(300-250) = 11,932 hr. Maximum likelihood estimates were b-hat = 1.024 and a-hat = 4.0e-4, giving intensity rho(t) = 0.00041 t^{0.024} and instantaneous MTTF = 1949 hr after 11,932 hr of testing. Using the idealized AMSAA growth model with initial cumulative M_I = 11,932/6 = 1988, final target M_F = 8000, t1 = 11,932 ~ 12,000, and total cumulative test time T_total = 36,000 hr, the growth parameter alpha was computed by approximation; alpha = 0.58 gave M(36,000) = 8951, then alpha was adjusted to 0.55 giving M(36,000) = 8083. Milestone targets at 6000-hr increments using the idealized growth curve with alpha=0.55 were:
Cumulative test time (hr) 18,000: M(t)=5521, cumulative failures 7.24, average interval MTTF 4966
24,000: M=6468, cumulative failures 8.24, avg interval MTTF 5992
30,000: M=7312, cumulative failures 9.11, avg interval MTTF 6889
36,000: M=8083, cumulative failures 9.9, avg interval MTTF 7697
State:
a) the MLEs a-hat and b-hat and the intensity rho(t);
b) the total cumulative test time after Type I completion;
c) the adjusted growth parameter alpha used and resulting M(36,000);
d) the milestone M(t) values at 18,000, 24,000, 30,000, and 36,000 hr.

