An original equipment manufacturer tested 15 oxygen sensors from supplier 1 at a high temperature. The 15 failure times (in hours) for supplier 1 (complete exact data) are:
170, 205, 207, 240, 275, 285, 324, 328, 334, 352, 385, 479, 500, 607, 701.

The lognormal distribution adequately fits these life data. Using the lognormal model, estimate:
(a) the life distribution parameters (i.e., the lognormal parameters μ and σ for ln(lifetime));
(b) the population fraction failing by 200 hours, P(T ≤ 200);
(c) the median life.

An exponentially failing large electronic system showed r = 20 failures in a total operated time T = 2000 hours, with the 20th failure occurring exactly at T = 2000 hours when observation was terminated. Find a 95% confidence interval for the true mean time between failures. Use the following formulas for the two-sided confidence limits for the mean m = T/r based on a chi-square distribution with 2r degrees of freedom:

m = T / r

Lower limit L = (2 r m) / χ^2_{α/2; 2r}

Upper limit U = (2 r m) / χ^2_{1-α/2; 2r}

For 1 − α = 0.95, α/2 = 0.025 and 1 − α/2 = 0.975, and for 2r = 40 degrees of freedom, the chi-square percentage points from tables are:

χ^2_{0.025; 40} = 59.3 and χ^2_{0.975; 40} = 24.4

Three identical units operate in parallel to form a vital link. Each unit consists of a number of components in series whose total failure rate is λ = 0.01 per hour. For a single unit the reliability over time t is R = e^{-λ t}. For n units in parallel the unreliability of the parallel group is Q_p = Q^n where Q = 1 - R, and the reliability of the parallel group is R_p = 1 - Q_p = 1 - (1 - e^{-λ t})^n. The mean time between failures of the n-unit parallel group (nonmaintained, with failures occurring until all n units have failed) is m_p = 1/λ + 1/(2λ) + ... + 1/(nλ).

Using λ = 0.01 per hour, n = 3, and t = 10 hours, compute:
a) the reliability of a single unit for 10 hours, R;
b) the unreliability of a single unit for 10 hours, Q;
c) the unreliability of the three-unit parallel group for 10 hours, Q_p;
d) the reliability of the three-unit parallel group for 10 hours, R_p;
e) the mean time between failures of the three-unit parallel group, m_p (in hours);
f) the expected number of link failures in 1000 ten-hour operations (i.e., over 10,000 hours) if the group is not maintained between operations;
g) the average reliability per 10-hour operation for the nonmaintained group (successful operations / 1000).

Assume an equipment has mean time between random (chance) failures m = 4000 hours (so the chance-failure survival over time t is exponential R_chance(t) = e^{-t/m}). The scheduled overhaul period is T_o = 800 hours. Additionally, the equipment has a wearout-life distribution that is approximately normal with mean μ = 1200 hours and standard deviation σ = 100 hours. With the overhaul period fixed at T_o = 800 hours, determine:
(a) the probability that the equipment will fail due to wearout before 800 hours;
(b) the probability that the equipment will operate for 800 hours without any failure when both random failures (exponential, m = 4000) and wearout failures (normal, μ = 1200, σ = 100) are considered (i.e., the combined reliability at T_o = 800 h);
(c) the average time between overhauls T_avg = ∫_0^{T_o} R_total(t) dt where R_total(t) = R_chance(t) × (1 − F_wearout(t)), with F_wearout(t) the normal CDF for wearout times (use T_o = 800 h).

A pressure vessel is equipped with six relief valves. Pressure transients can be controlled successfully by any three of these valves (i.e., the system fails to control a pressure transient if four or more valves fail). The probability that any one valve will fail to operate on demand is 0.04, and valve failures are independent. Using the rare-event approximation for system unreliability

\widetilde{R}_{a} = 1 - R_{a} \approx C_{N-m+1}^{N} (1-R)^{\,N-m+1}

(where N is the total number of components, m is the number required for success, and 1-R is the single-component failure probability), find the probability on demand that the relief valve system will fail to control a pressure transient.

You are to design an m/N detection (voting/redundant) system using identical components. For each component the probability of fail to danger (failure to detect a real danger) is p_d and the probability of spurious operation (fail safe, i.e., falsely indicating danger) is p_s. For an m/N system the system fail-to-danger probability R_fd and the system fail-safe (spurious) probability R_fs are given by the binomial sums:

R_fd = sum_{n=N-m+1}^{N} C(N,n) p_d^n (1-p_d)^{N-n}

R_fs = sum_{n=m}^{N} C(N,n) p_s^n (1-p_s)^{N-n}

(For p_d << 1 or p_s << 1 these can be approximated by the leading term: R_fd ≈ C(N,N-m+1) p_d^{N-m+1} and R_fs ≈ C(N,m) p_s^m.)

Given:

p_d = 10^{-2}
p_s = 10^{-2}

Design requirements:

1. Probability of system fail to danger < 10^{-4}.
2. Probability of system fail safe < 10^{-2}.

Choose values of m and N with N as small as possible that meet both criteria.

Example 4.13: Find the 90% and the 95% two-sided confidence intervals for the mean of a sample of failures. The sample statistics (from Example 4.3) are: sample mean \bar{\mu} = 1303, sample standard deviation \hat{\sigma} = 661, and sample size N = 16. Use the normal-approximation formula for large-sample confidence intervals:

mu^{\pm} = \bar{\mu} \pm z_{\alpha/2} \frac{\hat{\sigma}}{\sqrt{N}}

and use z_{\alpha/2} = 1.645 for 90% confidence and z_{\alpha/2} = 1.960 for 95% confidence.

Using the exponential lower tolerance limit formula

L = (2 n x̄ ln(1 / P)) / χ^2_{2n,γ}

where χ^2_{2n,γ} is the γ-fractile of the χ^2-distribution with 2n degrees of freedom (so that with confidence γ a proportion P of items will have lifetimes of at least L), calculate the lower tolerance limit L given the following data: x̄ = 580.4, n = 31, P = 0.9, and χ^2_{62,0.095} = 81.3807.

Calculate a 95% lower tolerance limit L for lifetimes assumed to follow an exponential distribution, with confidence γ = 0.90, using the following formula for an exponential lower tolerance limit:

L = (2 n x̄ ln(1 / P)) / χ^2_{2n, γ}

where χ^2_{2n, γ} is the γ-fractile (90th percentile) of the chi-square distribution with 2n degrees of freedom. Use n = 31 and sample mean x̄ = 580.4, and set the coverage P = 0.95. Compute L numerically.

Assume that safety-related monthly problems in a mine over the period of seven months were 20, 5, 15, 10, 30, 25, and 12. Calculate the standard deviation σ of this data set, using the population standard deviation formula

σ = [ (Σ_{j=1}^k (y_j - μ)^2) / k ]^{1/2}

where μ is the mean and k is the number of data points. (Use the given seven data values to compute μ and then σ.)

